@*
    The reference fragment (task 3.1). Every later list view copies this shape.

    Four rules it exists to demonstrate, all of which the Phase 3 exit criterion checks:

      1. NO `style=` ATTRIBUTE, EVER. All styling is a class in wwwroot/css/dndump.css. A resync
         that changes a colour or a spacing edits that stylesheet's tokens and nothing here.
      2. The htmx contract is markup (SERVER.md §5.1): #v-{view} is the fragment's stable id and
         swaps outerHTML, #rows-{view} is the infinite-scroll append target. Both are derived from
         Model.View.Name so they cannot drift from the route.
      3. No conditionals beyond presence/absence, and no formatting logic. Anything cleverer than
         "render this value" belongs in DotNetDump.Web.Rendering.Display or the view model --
         a template a designer cannot safely re-generate is a template that has failed.
      4. The type column truncates in the middle, never at the end. See MiddleTruncated: the tail
         of a .NET type name is what distinguishes it, so cutting the tail loses the answer.

    Task 4.1-4.3 adds the filter bar, active-filter chips and sortable headers, all inside one
    <form> -- see _FilterBar.cshtml's own doc comment for why the form is what makes
    hx-include="closest form" preserve a filter across a sort click and vice versa.
    HeapAnalyzer.GetHeapStatistics only recognizes "count" and "typename" as SortBy values (anything
    else, including no value, falls back to TotalSize -- there is no dedicated string for that
    default), so MethodTable and Total size stay plain, unsortable headers.

    Task 4.4 pulls the <tr> loop out into _DumpHeapRows.cshtml so GET /views/dumpheap/rows can
    render the same row markup (plus a fresh sentinel row) without this fragment's <table>/<thead>
    around it. The "no data at all" vs. "no rows match the filter" distinction below stays here,
    not in the partial, because it only makes sense on the page this fragment renders.
*@
@model ListModel<HeapStatItem>
@{
    var filterBar = FilterBar.Build(Model.View, Context.Request.Query);
    var countSort = SortHeader.For(Model.View, Context.Request.Query, "count", SortDirection.Desc);
    var typeSort = SortHeader.For(Model.View, Context.Request.Query, "typename", SortDirection.Asc);
}

<div id="v-@Model.View.Name">
    <form class="dn-filterform">
        <partial name="/Views/Shared/_FilterBar.cshtml" model="@filterBar" />
        <table class="dn-table">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="col" class="dn-th dn-th--mono">MethodTable</th>
                    <th scope="col" class="dn-th dn-th--num" aria-sort="@countSort.AriaSort">
                        <a class="dn-th__sort" hx-get="@countSort.Href" hx-target="#v-@Model.View.Name" hx-push-url="true" hx-include="closest form">Count@(countSort.Indicator)</a>
                    </th>
                    <th scope="col" class="dn-th dn-th--num">Total size</th>
                    <th scope="col" class="dn-th" aria-sort="@typeSort.AriaSort">
                        <a class="dn-th__sort" hx-get="@typeSort.Href" hx-target="#v-@Model.View.Name" hx-push-url="true" hx-include="closest form">Type@(typeSort.Indicator)</a>
                    </th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody id="rows-@Model.View.Name">
                <partial name="/Views/Fragments/_DumpHeapRows.cshtml" model="@Model" />

                @if (Model.Result.Items.Count == 0)
                {
                    <tr>
                        <td colspan="4" class="dn-empty">
                            @* Three different empty states are distinguished, per DESIGN_BRIEF.md §5:
                               an unfiltered empty result means the dump holds nothing of this kind,
                               whereas a filtered one means the filter is too narrow -- and telling a
                               user "no data" when they have simply over-filtered sends them looking
                               for a problem in the dump. *@
                            @if (Model.Result.TotalUnfiltered > 0)
                            {
                                <div class="dn-empty__title">No rows match the current filter.</div>
                                <a class="dn-btn" href="@(filterBar.ClearAllUrl ?? $"/views/{Model.View.Name}")">Clear filters</a>
                            }
                            else
                            {
                                <div class="dn-empty__title">This dump has no heap statistics to show.</div>
                            }
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </form>
</div>
